# SYNX — how git should treat what is in here.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# The working tree arrived with mixed line endings: about half the JavaScript
# and half the Rust was CRLF and the rest LF, with no pattern to it beyond
# which editor happened to touch a file last. That is invisible until two
# people work on the same file, at which point one commit rewrites every line
# of it and the diff is useless for review.
#
# So endings are normalised in the repository and left to the platform in the
# working tree. `text=auto eol=lf` means: decide text vs binary by content,
# store text with LF, and check it out as LF everywhere. Windows tooling -
# Node, cargo, rustc, every editor in current use - reads LF perfectly well,
# and the alternative (`eol=crlf`) would corrupt the shell scripts.
#
# NOTE FOR THE FIRST COMMIT: normalisation applies when a file is next staged.
# If the tree is already committed with mixed endings, run
#
#     git add --renormalize .
#
# once, and commit the result on its own so the noise is in one commit rather
# than smeared through the next ten.

* text=auto eol=lf

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ code ---
*.rs      text eol=lf diff=rust
*.js      text eol=lf
*.mjs     text eol=lf
*.json    text eol=lf
*.html    text eol=lf diff=html
*.css     text eol=lf diff=css
*.md      text eol=lf
*.toml    text eol=lf
*.yml     text eol=lf
*.yaml    text eol=lf
*.sh      text eol=lf

# --------------------------------------------------------------- binary ----
# Declared explicitly rather than left to content detection. A false "text"
# guess on any of these rewrites bytes on checkout, and the failure mode is a
# game that will not start - the pack and the scene are not recoverable from a
# corrupted copy, and neither is the compiled core.
*.pak     binary
*.bin     binary
*.wasm    binary
*.png     binary
*.jpg     binary
*.jpeg    binary
*.gif     binary
*.ico     binary
*.webp    binary
*.mp3     binary
*.ogg     binary
*.wav     binary
*.ttf     binary
*.otf     binary
*.woff    binary
*.woff2   binary

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ lfs ----
# web/data/synx.pak is 56 MB and web/data/scene.bin is 11 MB. Both are tracked
# on purpose - the game does not start without them - and the pack is past the
# 50 MB point where GitHub starts warning and near the 100 MB point where it
# refuses the push outright.
#
# BEFORE THE FIRST PUSH to a GitHub remote, install Git LFS and run:
#
#     git lfs install
#     git lfs track "web/data/*.pak" "web/data/*.bin"
#     git add .gitattributes
#
# which appends the filter lines below this comment. Doing it AFTER a push
# means rewriting history, so it is worth the two minutes now. A host with no
# file-size limit needs none of this.

# ------------------------------------------------------------- linguist ----
# The pack and the scene are assets, not source; without this they dominate
# the language statistics and the repository is reported as "Other".
web/data/*      linguist-vendored
web/wasm/*      linguist-vendored
Cargo.lock      linguist-generated
